# def.dimensional-decomposition.history.morphological-analysis

## .what
dimensional decomposition = an application of morphological analysis (Zwicky box / morphological box)
a structured method to explore every possible configuration of a multi-dimensional problem

## .origin
Fritz Zwicky (1898–1974), Swiss-American astrophysicist at Caltech, developed it across the 1940s–60s as a general method for problem-solution and invention; applied to astrophysics + jet/rocket propulsion
sources:
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fritz_Zwicky
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morphological_analysis_(problem-solving)
- Zwicky, F. (1969). Discovery, Invention, Research Through the Morphological Approach. Macmillan.

## .core idea
break a problem into independent parameters (dimensions), list the values each can take, examine every combination
the "box" = the multi-dimensional matrix of all parameters and their options

## .the method (Zwicky's steps)
1. define the problem precisely
2. identify the parameters (key independent characteristics)
3. list possible values for each parameter
4. form the matrix (each parameter an axis)
5. generate configurations (one value per parameter = a candidate)
6. evaluate and filter — discard impossible/contradictory combos (cross-consistency assessment), assess survivors

## .worked example — Zwicky's "vehicle"
| parameter | values |
|-----------|--------|
| medium | land, water, air |
| power source | electric, combustion, human, solar |
| support | wheels, tracks, hull, wings |
| passengers | 1, 2–4, 5+ |
air/solar/wings/1 → a solar-powered single-seat aircraft; 3×4×4×3 = 144 configurations

## .why useful
- completeness — the entire solution space, not just obvious ideas
- less bias — counters the jump to familiar answers
- novelty — unexpected combos point to new inventions

## .combinatorial-explosion caveat
combinations grow multiplicatively; the cross-consistency assessment (discard pairs that cannot coexist) prunes the box to a workable set
this is the ancestor of our forbidden verdict — a cell marked impossible, with its reason

## .later development
Tom Ritchey + Swedish Morphological Society formalized computer-aided general morphological analysis (GMA) and cross-consistency assessment (CCA) for non-quantified problems
- Ritchey, T. (1998). General Morphological Analysis. https://www.swemorph.com/ma.html

## .maps to our practice
| morphological analysis | our decomposition |
|------------------------|-------------------|
| parameter | axis |
| value | axis value |
| Zwicky box | the product matrix |
| configuration | a cell |
| cross-consistency assessment | the forbidden verdict |
| survivor configuration | a filled or gap cell |

## .see also
- howto.dimensional-decomposition.md — how to apply it
- rule.require.dimensional-decomposition.md — when it is required
